        "additional_kwargs": {
          "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze what I have so far:\n\n1. **Alert**: log_loss = 0.5435 (observed) vs threshold 0.5412, reference 0.5067. Alert triggered because observed > threshold.\n\n2. **Context**: performance_history shows:\n   - \"focus\" window: log_loss 0.5435, alert true (this is the alert window)\n   - \"confirmation-1\" window: log_loss 0.5380, alert false\n   - \"confirmation-2\" window: log_loss 0.5130 (partially visible), alert false\n\n3. **Metrics**: confirms the alert metrics.\n\n4. **confirmation_1_metrics**: log_loss 0.5380, normal.\n\n5. **confirmation_2_metrics**: log_loss 0.5130, normal.\n\n6. **model_catalog**: reference_model and active_model have the SAME version (sha256:cbf03ab...). So no model version change.\n\n7. **predictions**: model_version is sha256:cbf03ab... - same as both models in catalog.\n\nKey observations:\n- The model version is the same in reference_model and active_model → not a deployment_regression due to model version change.\n- The alert window (focus) has log_loss 0.5435, but confirmation-1 has 0.5380 and confirmation-2 has 0.5130. Both below threshold. So the alert might be transient or specific to the focus window.\n- The focus window alert was triggered but confirmation windows are normal. This suggests the alert might not be a persistent degradation.\n\nWait, but the task is to investigate THIS alert. The alert is about the focus window. Let me think about what could cause it.\n\nPossible causes:\n- data_corruption: data got corrupted\n- data_drift: input distribution changed\n- concept_drift: relationship between features and target changed\n- deployment_regression: model deployment issue\n- no_incident: it's a false alarm / transient\n- undetermined\n\nKey findings:\n1. Model version unchanged (reference_model == active_model). So not a model version swap.\n2. Confirmation windows show normal performance (below threshold). This suggests the focus window anomaly might be isolated.\n\nLet me dig deeper. I need to check:\n- Whether the source data vs inputs data differ (data corruption at ingestion) - compare_tables source vs inputs\n- Whether input distribution drifted vs reference - compare_distributions source vs reference_clients\n- Whether concept drift - compare_conditional_relationship\n- Whether predictions match between models - but models are same version\n- Data quality checks\n\nLet me think about what's most useful. The fact that confirmation-1 and confirmation-2 are normal while focus is alerting is interesting. It could be:\n- A transient data issue in the focus window\n- Data drift specific to focus window\n\nLet me check:\n1. compare_tables source vs inputs - to see if data was corrupted between source and model input\n2. compare_distributions source vs reference_clients - to see data drift\n3. compare_conditional_relationship - to see concept drift\n\nAlso, I should verify the predictions/feedback with evaluate_predictions to confirm the log_loss.\n\nLet me first do the independent checks:\n- compare_tables(left=\"source\", right=\"inputs\") - data integrity\n- compare_distributions(left=\"source\", right=\"reference_clients\") - data drift\n- evaluate_predictions - verify metrics\n\nThese are independent, so I can do them together.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The compare_tables with key customer_id - source and inputs both have customer_id. Let me check if they differ.\n\nLet me also think about the comparison direction. compare_tables(left, right) - left/right gives direction of ratio. For data integrity, I want to see if source == inputs.\n\nLet me run these three in parallel:\n1. compare_tables(left=\"source\", right=\"inputs\", key=\"customer_id\")\n2. compare_distributions(left=\"source\", right=\"reference_clients\") - exclude customer_id\n3. evaluate_predictions(predictions=\"predictions\", feedback=\"feedback\")\n\nThese are all independent.",
